module

public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Presheaf.NoncommBaseChange
public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Sites.LocallySurjective

/-!
# The sections of a pullback of presheaves of modules are a base change

Let `α : R ⟶ R'` be a morphism of presheaves of not necessarily commutative rings on a
category `C`. Mathlib's `PresheafOfModules.pullback` is defined as a left adjoint of the
pushforward and carries no description of its sections, which is what every consumer of it
actually needs. `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.Presheaf.extendScalarsIsoPullback` already
identifies that abstract left adjoint, along the identity functor of `C`, with the objectwise
extension of scalars `V ↦ R'(V) ⊗_{R(V)} M(V)`. This file turns that isomorphism of functors
into the statements about *sections* that consumers use:

* the sections over an object are the base change, and the adjoint transpose of a morphism
  `θ : M ⟶ (restrictScalars α).obj N` is, on the pure tensor `b ⊗ m`, the element `b • θ(m)`;
* consequently, when `α` is surjective on sections over `V`, the adjoint transpose and `θ`
  have the *same* image over `V`. So a surjective comparison map forces the `θ` it is the
  transpose of to be surjective, and a comparison map that is an isomorphism exhibits the
  sections of `N` as a base change of the sections of `M`.

## Scope: the fixed site, and what is deliberately not re-proved here

Everything below is for a **change of rings on a fixed site**: the functor of sites is the
identity functor of `C`, and `α : R ⟶ R'` is a morphism of presheaves of rings on that one
category. It is *not* for a general `φ : S ⟶ F.op ⋙ R` over a functor `F : C ⥤ D`. The
restriction is not a convenience: for a general `F` the pullback is extension of scalars
followed by the objectwise filtered colimit `V ↦ colim_{W, V ⟶ F W} (-)(W)` (the second
factor is the subject of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Presheaf.PullbackColimit`), and there is no candidate
of the simple shape `R'(V) ⊗_{R(V)} M(V)` to compare the sections with.

The *generation* half of the base-change description **is** available for an arbitrary `F`,
with no hypotheses, and is **not re-derived here**:

* `PresheafOfModules.range_homEquivSymm_app_eq_generatedSubmodule` — the image of the adjoint
  transpose of `ψ : P ⟶ (pushforward φ).obj Q` is, over every object, the submodule generated
  by the restrictions of the image of `ψ`;
* `PresheafOfModules.generatedSubmodule_unit_eq_top` — hence `(pullback φ).obj P` is generated
  by the image of the unit.

Both live in `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Crystalline.SmallSite.ComparisonSurjectivity`. No
statement here restates either of them, and nothing here is proved by re-running the
generation argument.

What this file adds to them is the two things that module says it does not have.

* **The injectivity half, for the identity functor, unconditionally.** That module records
  that the adjunction determines the image of a transpose "and nothing else", so that it
  "does not produce an isomorphism", the missing half being injectivity. For the identity
  functor the isomorphism is nevertheless available with no hypothesis — no quasi-coherence,
  no flatness, no presentation of `M` — because the objectwise base change is itself a left
  adjoint of the same pushforward, so the comparison of the two left adjoints is an
  isomorphism by uniqueness rather than by an argument about sections. That is
  `pullbackObjIso`, and `transpose_toPullbackObj_objTmul` is the computation rule that makes
  it usable. A consumer that needs to *invert* a canonical map out of a pullback, and whose
  map of sites is the identity, is unblocked by this.
* **The collapse of the generated submodule to the image.** That module's own account of what
  separates it from the surjectivity statement [Stacks, Tag 07JN] consumes begins: "the
  submodule generated by the image of the `f`-map collapses to that image only when the ring
  map `𝒪_{T'}(W') ⟶ 𝒪_T(f^{-1}W')` is surjective". That collapse is proved here, as
  `exists_apply_eq_transpose_apply` and its two corollaries, under exactly that hypothesis
  and for the identity functor. For a general `F` the collapse is false as stated: the
  generators over an object `Y` are restrictions along morphisms `F(X) ⟶ Y`, and such a
  restriction is of the form `ψ(s')` again only when `Y` is itself `F(X)`.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.Presheaf.toPullbackObj`: the canonical map
  `R'(V) ⊗_{R(V)} M(V) ⟶ (f^*M)(V)` from the base change of the sections to the sections of
  Mathlib's `PresheafOfModules.pullback`.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.Presheaf.pullbackObjIso`: **the sections of
  `PresheafOfModules.pullback` over an object are the base change of the sections**,
  `(f^*M)(V) ≅ R'(V) ⊗_{R(V)} M(V)`. No hypotheses.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.Presheaf.objIsoOfIsIso`: for a morphism `θ` whose adjoint
  transpose is an isomorphism, **the sections of the target are a base change of the sections
  of the source**, `N(V) ≅ R'(V) ⊗_{R(V)} M(V)`.
* `CategoryTheory.Adjunction.leftAdjointUniq_hom_app_homEquiv_symm`: the comparison of two
  left adjoints of one functor intertwines their adjoint transposes.

## Main results

* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.Presheaf.transpose_toPullbackObj_objTmul`: the adjoint
  transpose of `θ`, read on the pure tensor `b ⊗ m` of the sections, is `b • θ(m)`. This is
  the computation rule the abstract left adjoint does not provide, and everything below is a
  consequence of it.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.Presheaf.exists_apply_eq_transpose_apply`: **the collapse of
  the generated submodule to the image.** When `α` is surjective on sections over `V`, every
  value of the adjoint transpose over `V` is already a value of `θ` over `V` — the two maps
  have the same image, and not merely the same generated submodule.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.Presheaf.surjective_of_surjective_transpose`: **a surjective
  comparison map forces the map it is the transpose of to be surjective**, over any object
  where `α` is surjective on sections.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.Presheaf.isLocallySurjective_of_isLocallySurjective_transpose`:
  the same implication for a Grothendieck topology on `C`, with "surjective" replaced by
  "locally surjective" on the two comparison maps and `α` still surjective on sections.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.objTmul_eq_of_surjective` and
  `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.exists_apply_eq_desc_apply`: the ring-theoretic input. When
  `f : A →+* B` is surjective, `B ⊗_A M` is the image of `M` under `m ↦ 1 ⊗ m`, so a map out
  of the base change has the same image as the semilinear map it descends.

## What is and is not proved

The hypotheses, exactly:

* `pullbackObjIso`, `toPullbackObj_comp_pullbackTranspose`,
  `transpose_toPullbackObj_objTmul` and `surjective_toPullbackObj` carry **no hypothesis**
  beyond the standing one that the functor of sites is the identity. In particular they do not
  need quasi-coherence of `M`, flatness of `α`, a presentation of `M`, affineness, smallness
  of `C`, or a topology on `C`. That is worth stating because the general-`F` injectivity
  question does need such a hypothesis: here it is avoided because the comparison is an
  isomorphism of left adjoints of one and the same functor, not a computation on sections.
* `objIsoOfIsIso` and `objIsoOfIsIso_hom_objTmul` assume the adjoint transpose of `θ` is an
  isomorphism of presheaves of modules, and nothing else.
* `exists_apply_eq_transpose_apply`, `surjective_of_surjective_transpose` and
  `isLocallySurjective_of_isLocallySurjective_transpose` assume that `α` is surjective on
  sections over the object in question — over every object, for the local statement. That
  hypothesis is genuinely needed and is not a convenience; see the implementation notes for
  the counterexample without it. `surjective_transpose_of_surjective`, the converse, needs
  nothing.
* `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.exists_apply_eq_desc_apply` and `surjective_desc_iff` assume
  the ring homomorphism is surjective, and nothing about the modules.

What is **not** proved, and is false, is the corresponding statement for
`SheafOfModules.pullback`. The pullback of sheaves of modules is the *sheafification* of the
presheaf pullback (Mathlib's `SheafOfModules.pullbackIso`, and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.ChangeOfRings.extendScalarsIsoPullback` for the identity functor), and
the sheafification unit of `V ↦ R'(V) ⊗_{R(V)} M(V)` is not an isomorphism for a general `M`:
inverting it is exactly where flatness or a presentation of `M` has to enter. Accordingly
nothing here asserts that the sections of a sheaf-level pullback are a base change. The
sheaf-level consequence that *is* available with no extra hypothesis is the local one,
`isLocallySurjective_of_isLocallySurjective_transpose`: sheafification does not change local
surjectivity, so a locally surjective comparison map at the sheaf level is a locally
surjective comparison map at the presheaf level, and the theorem applies to it.

Also **not** proved is the injectivity half for a general functor of sites, which is the case
the crystalline comparison `c_f` needs when the map on ambient schemes is not the identity:
there `f^{-1}` is a filtered colimit and the sections of the pullback over `V` are
`colim_{W, V ⊆ f^{-1}W} (𝒪_T(V) ⊗_{𝒪_{T'}(W)} F(W))`, not a single tensor product. The
present file says nothing about that colimit, and nothing here should be read as covering it;
the factorisation of the pullback into extension of scalars and that colimit is
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Presheaf.PullbackColimit`, and the generation half,
valid for every `F`, is the module cited above. A hypothesis of quasi-coherence on `M`
would be the natural way in for the general case, and is not used anywhere here — for the
identity functor no such hypothesis is needed, and for a general `F` nothing is claimed.

Nothing here carries a `@[stacks]` attribute. Neither the description of the sections of a
pullback nor the surjectivity transfer is a tagged statement of the source; both are
auxiliaries, used in the proof of [Stacks, Tag 07JN] and in the construction of the canonical
connection of [Stacks, Tag 07J6], rather than content of any tag.

## Implementation notes

**Why `α` must be surjective on sections.** Without it the conclusion fails outright: take
`R` the constant presheaf `ℤ`, `R'` the constant presheaf `ℚ`, `M = R` and `N = R'`. The
transpose of the unit `θ : ℤ ⟶ ℚ` is the identity of `ℚ ⊗_ℤ ℤ = ℚ`, which is surjective,
while `θ` is not. What surjectivity of `α` buys is that the image of `θ` over `V`, which is
by semilinearity an `R(V)`-submodule of `N(V)`, is already an `R'(V)`-submodule, and the image
of the transpose is the `R'(V)`-submodule it generates.

**The two spellings of a pure tensor.** As in
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.NoncommExtendScalars` and
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Presheaf.NoncommBaseChange`, a pure tensor of the
sections is written `Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.objTmul`, whose ascribed type is
`↑((extendScalars f).obj M)`, and a morphism into a restriction of scalars is applied through
`Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.ofRestrict`. The underlying type of the base change,
`Stacks.NoncommChangeOfRings.BaseChange`, is definitionally equal but syntactically different,
and a `rw` across the two reports the goal as not type-correct. For that reason the induction
over pure tensors is done once and for all at the `BaseChange` level, in
`exists_apply_eq_desc_apply`, and the presheaf-level statements are deduced from it by
rewriting with the computation rule rather than by a second induction.

**The comparison of two left adjoints.** `extendScalarsIsoPullback` is
`Adjunction.leftAdjointUniq` of the objectwise adjunction and Mathlib's pullback adjunction.
Mathlib records how `leftAdjointUniq` interacts with the units and the counits of the two
adjunctions, but not with their hom-set bijections, which is the form needed to transport a
computation of the adjoint transpose from one left adjoint to the other.
`CategoryTheory.Adjunction.leftAdjointUniq_hom_app_homEquiv_symm` supplies that, from
`Adjunction.leftAdjointUniq_hom_app_counit` and naturality.

